The tuple contents include A s , the self-destruct set: a set of accounts that will be discarded following the transaction's completion. A l is the log series: this is a series of archived and indexable 'checkpoints' in VM code execution that allow for contract-calls to be easily tracked by onlookers external to the Ethereum world (such as decentralised application front-ends). A t is the set of touched accounts, of which the empty ones are deleted at the end of a transaction. A r is the refund balance, increased through using the SSTORE instruction in order to reset contract storage to zero from some non-zero value. Though not immediately refunded, it is allowed to partially offset the total execution costs. Finally, EIP-2929 by Buterin and Swende [2020a] introduced A a , the set of accessed account addresses, and A K , the set of accessed storage keys (more accurately, each element of A K is a tuple of a 20-byte account address and a 32-byte storage slot).

