## 6.  USING TIME TO  MAKE REQUESTS

A  client need  not  explicitly send  a message to  make  a request. Not receiving a  request can trigger execution of a  command-in effect, allowing the passage of time to transmit a request from client to  state  machine [Lamport 19841.  Transmitting a  request using time instead of  messages  can be advantageous because  protocols that implement ICl and  IC2  can  be  costly both in total number of  messages exchanged and  in delay. Unfortunately, using time to  transmit requests has  only limited applicability since the client cannot specify parameter values.

