## 2.5 Random Surfer Model

The de#0Cnition of PageRank above has another intuitive basis in random walks on graphs. The simpli#0Ced version corresponds to the standing probability distribution of a random walk on the graph of the Web. Intuitively, this can be thought of as modeling the behavior of a #5Crandom surfer"". The #5Crandom surfer"" simply keeps clicking on successive links at random. However, if a real Web surfer ever gets into a small loop of web pages, it is unlikely that the surfer will continue in the loop forever. Instead, the surfer will jump to some other page. The additional factor E can be viewed as a way of modeling this behavior: the surfer periodically #5Cgets bored"" and jumps to a random page chosen based on the distribution in E .

So far we have left E as a user de#0Cned parameter. In most tests we let E be uniform over all web pages with value #0B . However, in Section 6 we show how di#0Berent values of E can generate #5Ccustomized"" page ranks.

