## 5 Searching with PageRank

A major application of PageRank is searching. We have implemented two search engines which use PageRank. The #0Crst one we will discuss is a simple title-based search engine. The second search engine is a full text search engine called Google #5BBP#5D. Google utilizes a number of factors to rank search results including standard IR measures, proximity, anchor text #28text of links pointing to web pages#29, and PageRank. While a comprehensive user study of the bene#0Cts of PageRank is beyond the scope of this paper, we have performed some comparative experiments and provide some sample results in this paper.

The bene#0Cts of PageRank are the greatest for underspeci#0Ced queries. For example, a query for #5CStanford University"" may return any number of web pages which mention Stanford #28such as publication lists#29 on a conventional search engine, but using PageRank, the university home page is listed #0Crst.

## 5.1 Title Search

To test the usefulness of PageRank for search we implemented a search engine that used only the titles of 16 million web pages. To answer a query, the search engine #0Cnds all the web pages whose titles contain all of the query words. Then it sorts the results by PageRank. This search engine is very simple and cheap to implement. In informal tests, it worked remarkably well. As can be seen in Figure 6, a search for #5CUniversity"" yields a list of top universities. This #0Cgure shows our MultiQuery system which allows a user to query two search engines at the same time. The search engine on the left is our PageRank based title search engine. The bar graphs and percentages shown are a log of the actual PageRank with the top page normalized to 100#25, not a percentile which is used everywhere else in this paper. The search engine on the right is Altavista. You can see that Altavista returns random looking web pages that match the query #5CUniversity"" and are the root page of the server #28Altavista seems to be using URL length as a quality heuristic#29.

