At high concurrency levels, LCRQ outperforms our wait-free queues in the 50% -enqueues benchmark by about 50% on Intel Haswell and 35% on AMD Magny-Cours; this is because our waitfree queue is more costly when trying to dequeue from an empty queue. To return an empty value, a dequeue needs to inspect its enqueue peer first which elevates traffic between cores. Moreover, when a dequeue cannot return empty because enqueues have performed FAA but have yet to put values in the queue, the dequeue will consume more cells before it completes.

