3. How does the fast-path/slow-path design guarantee progress without making common operations too expensive?,A Wait-free Queue as Fast as Fetch-and-Add (2016),"Based on the wait-free queue paper (""A Wait-free Queue as Fast as Fetch-and-Add"", 2016), which uses Kogan and Petrank's fast-path–slow-path methodology, the design reconciles strong progress with cheap common-case operations as follows:

**1. Two separate implementations per operation**
Each queue operation has two versions:
- A **fast path**, which is a simple, efficient algorithm — in this paper, the ordinary obstruction-free fetch-and-add (FAA)-based enqueue/dequeue. This is what runs in the common case and is very cheap.
- A **slow path**, which is a more involved but wait-free implementation. It is more expensive, so it is avoided unless needed.

**2. The common case never pays for the slow path**
An operation first attempts only its fast path. It retries the fast path until it either succeeds or the number of failures exceeds a fixed threshold (called `PATIENCE`). As long as the operation succeeds within that budget — which is the normal case — it never touches the slow-path machinery at all. So common operations pay only the cost of the simple fast algorithm. The paper sets `PATIENCE` to 10, ""a practical threshold that should allow most operations to complete on the fast-path, maximizing performance."" The memory-reclamation scheme is also designed so that it ""adds no memory fence along common execution paths in queue operations,"" further keeping the fast path cheap.

**3. Progress comes from a bounded fallback to a helping-based slow path**
Progress (wait-freedom) is guaranteed because the number of fast-path attempts is bounded, and once the threshold is exceeded the operation switches to the slow path, which is constructed to always complete in a finite number of steps. The slow path works by *helping*: an operation publishes/announces its request, and other threads help complete pending requests. In the queue paper, each dequeuer keeps a pointer to a ""dequeue peer""; whenever a thread successfully completes an operation, it helps its peer's pending request. The proof sketch shows an enqueue attempts at most `PATIENCE` times on the fast path, and on the slow path ""every time the enqueuer fails, some thread will update its peer to the next thread""; after at most (n−1)² failures, all other threads become its helpers and it completes. Hence every operation finishes within a bounded number of steps — wait-freedom, not merely lock-freedom.

**4. The expensive part is only paid by operations that would otherwise starve**
The slow path is invoked only by operations that repeatedly fail, which happens only under contention. Thus the expensive helping machinery is paid precisely by the rare, contended operations, while the common path stays at the speed of the underlying fast algorithm. The empirical result confirms this: the wait-free queue's throughput under high contention is ""often as high as that of a microbenchmark that only performs FAA,"" disproving ""the myth that ensuring wait-freedom and linearizability together is too expensive to be efficient.""

In short: the fast path keeps common operations at the cost of a plain efficient algorithm, while the *bounded* retry budget plus a helping-based slow path guarantees that even the unluckiest operation finishes in a finite number of steps.","[{""tool"": ""search"", ""args"": {""query"": ""fast-path slow-path design guarantee progress common operations too expensive"", ""num_results"": 10}}, {""tool"": ""search"", ""args"": {""query"": ""fast-path-slow-path methodology wait-free queue PATIENCE threshold fast-path attempts slow-path progress"", ""num_results"": 10}}, {""tool"": ""search"", ""args"": {""query"": ""fast-path attempts threshold switch slow-path helping mechanism guarantee finite number of attempts wait-freedom"", ""num_results"": 10}}, {""tool"": ""search"", ""args"": {""query"": ""wait-free stack fast-path slow-path eliminates overhead common case operations attempts"", ""num_results"": 8}}]",non_blocking_algorithms__a-wait-free-queue-as-fast-as-fetch-and-add.json,"## Abstract

Concurrent data structures that have fast and predictable performance are of critical importance for harnessing the power of multicore processors, which are now ubiquitous. Although wait-free objects, whose operations complete in a bounded number of steps, were devised more than two decades ago, wait-free objects that can deliver scalable high performance are still rare.

In this paper, we present the first wait-free FIFO queue based on fetch-and-add (FAA). While compare-and-swap (CAS) based non-blocking algorithms may perform poorly due to work wasted by CAS failures, algorithms that coordinate using FAA, which is guaranteed to succeed, can in principle perform better under high contention. Along with FAA, our queue uses a custom epoch-based scheme to reclaim memory; on x86 architectures, it requires no extra memory fences on our algorithm's typical execution path. An empirical study of our new FAA-based wait-free FIFO queue under high contention on four different architectures with many hardware threads shows that it outperforms prior queue designs that lack a wait-free progress guarantee. Surprisingly, at the highest level of contention, the throughput of our queue is often as high as that of a microbenchmark that only performs FAA. As a result, our fast waitfree queue implementation is useful in practice on most multi-core systems today. We believe that our design can serve as an example of how to construct other fast wait-free objects.

