"4. Why does the queue use epoch-based reclamation and hazard pointers, and what overhead does that avoid on x86?",A Wait-free Queue as Fast as Fetch-and-Add (2016),"Based on the paper *""A Wait-free Queue as Fast as Fetch-and-Add""* (Yang & Mellor-Crummey, 2016), the queue uses a custom reclamation scheme that is essentially **epoch-based reclamation** (originally proposed by Harris for non-blocking linked lists) combined with **hazard pointers**.

## Why this combination is used

The only garbage the wait-free queue needs to reclaim is **segments that are no longer in use**. A segment `segment[i]` is retired once both the tail and head have moved past `i × N` and every enqueued value in it has been dequeued.

- Each thread's handle carries a **hazard pointer (`hzdp`)** that announces the segment the thread is currently using: a thread writes the head or tail pointer it is using into `hzdp` at the start of an operation and clears it when the operation completes.
- The queue structure also keeps a 64-bit integer `I` recording the id of the oldest segment in the queue.
- Periodically, a ""cleaning"" thread scans every thread's head, tail, and hazard pointer to find the earliest segment still in use and reclaims all preceding (retired) segments.

So the hazard pointers tell the reclaimer *which segments are still live*, and the epoch/grace-period machinery (with the backward-traversal step to handle ""backward jumps"" when a thread helps a peer's dequeue) determines *when* a retired segment is safe to free.

## What overhead this avoids on x86

On a memory model weaker than sequential consistency, a **memory fence** is normally needed after a thread sets its hazard pointer. However:

- On **x86**, atomic primitives flush store buffers, so **no extra memory fence is needed** after setting the hazard pointer.
- Because the queue performs a **fetch-and-add (FAA)** immediately after setting the hazard pointer (using the head or tail pointer), the enqueue and dequeue fast paths need **no extra fence** at all.
- Only the `help_deq` path requires a memory fence after setting the hazard pointer.

The paper states that, as a result, on x86 the reclamation scheme ""adds almost no overhead to the fast-path execution, which is unprecedented among memory reclamation schemes for lock-free data structures.""

By contrast, on weakly ordered **Power** architectures, the protocol requires a full fence (`sync`) after setting a hazard pointer and a lightweight fence (`lwsync`) before clearing it — the same cost as other epoch-based reclamation strategies.","[{""tool"": ""search"", ""args"": {""query"": ""epoch-based reclamation hazard pointers queue overhead x86"", ""num_results"": 10}}, {""tool"": ""search"", ""args"": {""query"": ""wait-free queue memory reclamation segment hazard pointer epoch grace period"", ""num_results"": 10}}]",non_blocking_algorithms__a-wait-free-queue-as-fast-as-fetch-and-add.json,"## Abstract

Concurrent data structures that have fast and predictable performance are of critical importance for harnessing the power of multicore processors, which are now ubiquitous. Although wait-free objects, whose operations complete in a bounded number of steps, were devised more than two decades ago, wait-free objects that can deliver scalable high performance are still rare.

In this paper, we present the first wait-free FIFO queue based on fetch-and-add (FAA). While compare-and-swap (CAS) based non-blocking algorithms may perform poorly due to work wasted by CAS failures, algorithms that coordinate using FAA, which is guaranteed to succeed, can in principle perform better under high contention. Along with FAA, our queue uses a custom epoch-based scheme to reclaim memory; on x86 architectures, it requires no extra memory fences on our algorithm's typical execution path. An empirical study of our new FAA-based wait-free FIFO queue under high contention on four different architectures with many hardware threads shows that it outperforms prior queue designs that lack a wait-free progress guarantee. Surprisingly, at the highest level of contention, the throughput of our queue is often as high as that of a microbenchmark that only performs FAA. As a result, our fast waitfree queue implementation is useful in practice on most multi-core systems today. We believe that our design can serve as an example of how to construct other fast wait-free objects.

