"3. What makes guarded input different from ordinary input, especially inside alternatives and loops?","Programming Teclmiques S. L. Graham,  R. L. Rivest Editors","Based on Hoare's ""Communicating Sequential Processes"" (CSP) paper, here is the distinction.

## Ordinary input

An ordinary input command (e.g. `X?(x, y)`) appears in a sequential command list and specifies communication with another process. It is executed **when the flow of control reaches it**: the process blocks at that point until a *corresponding* output command is executed by the other process. Correspondence requires:

1. the input command names the other process as its source;
2. the other process's output command names the first process as its destination; and
3. the target variable matches the value denoted by the output expression.

When corresponding commands are found, they ""are executed simultaneously, and their combined effect is to assign the value of the expression of the output command to the target variable of the input command."" In other words, ordinary input is a blocking rendezvous fixed at a specific point in the program.

## Guarded input

A guarded input is an input command placed **inside a guard** (e.g. `X?(x) → ...`). It is not simply a blocking statement; it is a *condition for selecting* the guarded command. From the paper:

> ""Input commands may appear in guards. A guarded command with an input guard is selected for execution only if and when the source named in the input command is ready to execute the corresponding output command.""

> ""An input command at the end of a guard is executed only if and when a corresponding output command is executed.""

So the key difference is *when* the input happens: an ordinary input blocks once the program reaches it, whereas a guarded input makes the whole guarded command eligible only at the moment the matching output is ready — selection and communication are tied together.

## Inside alternatives

In an alternative command, guarded inputs give **nondeterministic selection among several possible communications** rather than committing to one blocking input:

> ""If several input guards of a set of alternatives have ready destinations, only one is selected and the others have no effect; but the choice between them is arbitrary.""

The alternative command ""specifies execution of exactly one of its constituent guarded commands,"" so the process waits on a *set* of possible inputs and proceeds with whichever becomes ready, instead of being stuck waiting on a single input statement. If no guard can succeed (e.g. all sources have terminated or Boolean guards fail), the alternative command fails.

## Inside loops (repetitive commands)

In a repetitive command, guarded inputs change both *selection* and *termination*:

> ""A repetitive command may have input guards. If all the sources named by them have terminated, then the repetitive command also terminates.""

This gives a clean way to write a server/consumer loop that repeatedly waits for any of several inputs and automatically terminates when all its input sources have ended — something an ordinary input inside a loop cannot detect, since a bare blocking input has no notion of ""all sources terminated.""","[{""tool"": ""search"", ""args"": {""query"": ""guarded input ordinary input alternatives loops"", ""num_results"": 10}}, {""tool"": ""search"", ""args"": {""query"": ""guarded input command ordinary input command alternatives repetitive command CSP"", ""num_results"": 10}}, {""tool"": ""search"", ""args"": {""query"": ""ordinary input command guarded command input guard Hoare"", ""num_results"": 10}}, {""tool"": ""search"", ""args"": {""query"": ""input command may appear in guard selected only if source ready to execute output command"", ""num_results"": 10}}, {""tool"": ""search"", ""args"": {""query"": ""input command output command executed simultaneously assign value target variable"", ""num_results"": 8}}]",processes__CSP-hoare.json,"Programming Teclmiques S. L. Graham,  R. L. Rivest Editors

## Communicating Sequential  Processes

C.A.R.  Hoare The Queen's University Belfast, Northern Ireland

This  paper  suggests  that  input  and output  are  basic primitives of programming  and that  parallel composition  of communicating sequential  processes  is  a fundamental program  structuring method.  When combined  with  a development  of Dijkstra's  guarded command,  these  concepts  are  surprisingly versatile. Their  use  is illustrated by sample  solutions  of a  variety of familiar  programming exercises. Key  Words  and  Phrases: programming,

programming languages,  programming  primitives, program structures, parallel  programming, concurrency, input,  output,  guarded commands,  nondeterminacy, coroutines,  procedures, multiple  entries,  multiple  exits, classes,  data  representations, recursion, conditional critical regions,  monitors, iterative arrays CR  Categories: 4.20,  4.22,  4.32

## 1.  Introduction

Among the primitive concepts of computer programming, and of the high level languages in which programs are  expressed,  the  action  of assignment  is  familiar  and well understood.  In fact, any change of the internal  state of a machine executing a program can be modeled as an assignment  of a  new value to some variable part  of that machine.  However, the  operations  of input  and  output, which affect the external environment  of a machine,  are not nearly  so well understood.  They are  often added  to a  programming  language  only as an afterthought. Among  the  structuring  methods  for  computer  pro-

